  Section 3 proof.

  I'll write it cleanly.

  Lemma 1 (border characterization). By definition.

  Lemma 2 (enumeration). The nested loops visit each ordered pair exactly once. Proof: outer index takes each value once; for fixed i inner takes each j once; distinct iterations have distinct (i,j) since values of loop counters determine the pair; total n·m iterations = number of pairs.

  Lemma 3 (invariant). After processing a set S of pairs (in loop order), sum = Σ_{(i,j)∈S} a_{ij}[P(i,j)]. Induction: initially S=∅, sum 0. Each step processes exactly one new pair (i,j), adds a_{ij} iff P(i,j) true; since [P] is 0/1 and the pair is new (not processed before), the equality is preserved. At termination S is all pairs.

  Theorem. sum = Σ_{all} a_{ij}[P] = Σ_{B} a_{ij}, each border cell once: each (i,j)∈B appears exactly once among the n·m enumerated pairs and its indicator is 1, hence contributes exactly a_{ij}; no other term contributes. Non-border: P false for all four disjuncts (i≠1, i≠n, j≠1, j≠m), so indicator 0, contributes nothing. Corners: satisfies two disjuncts but [P] is one boolean for the cell, and the cell is enumerated once, so no double count — this is precisely why we use a per-cell OR test instead of summing the four sides (which would count corners twice and require subtracting them).

  Consistency for degenerate dimensions: if n=1 then i=1 for every cell so P true for all; indeed every cell lies in row 1 (also row n). If m=1 similarly. If n=2, no row satisfies 1<i<n, so every cell is on row 1 or 2, P true for all; consistent. Same m=2. Thus the predicate implements the definition in all 1≤n,m≤500 without special-casing.

  Arithmetic correctness: Each a_{ij} ∈ [-1e7,1e7] and every partial sum is a sum of at most 1996 border values (actually at most |B| terms), so |partial| ≤ 1996·1e7 = 1.996e10 < 2^63−1; long long additions are exact, no overflow, so computed value equals mathematical sum.

